  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "In Sarah's batch of chocolate and vanilla cupcakes, $\\frac{3}{8}$ of the cupcakes are chocolate. If there are a total of $72$ cupcakes in the batch, how many vanilla cupcakes are there?",
  "worked_solution": "We are given that the ratio of chocolate cupcakes to the total number of cupcakes is $\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of chocolate cupcakes and the denominator represents the  total number of cupcakes.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $72$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{8\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $27$ chocolate cupcakes in the batch. Hence, the number of vanilla cupcakes is\n\n$$\n72-27=45.\n$$",
